# Fishing rod — the one item that must not read as `stick.wam`.
#
# `stick` is a brown tapered tube with a twig on it, and a fishing rod authored
# naively is exactly that tube with a slightly better taper. At 46px the two
# would be the same icon in two browns. So the model is built around the three
# things a stick cannot have, in the order they survive being shrunk:
#
#   1. **A hanging line with a float on the end.** This is the whole read. A
#      stick has nothing dangling off it. The bobber is a red-and-white ball
#      out in front of the pole with clear air on both sides, and it is the
#      first thing the eye resolves.
#   2. **A dark grip at the butt**, a hard colour break at a fifth of the way
#      up rather than a gradient. Two-tone along the length says "made object";
#      one tone says "branch".
#   3. **A reel** — a squat metal spool sticking out sideways off the top of
#      the grip. It is the only thing in the model that breaks the silhouette
#      sideways, which is what stops the rod reading as a single line.
#
# **The pole leans forward and that is load-bearing.** A dead-vertical rod puts
# the line straight down the pole's own axis: the line is then hidden behind
# the tube from every angle, and the bobber looks threaded onto the shaft. The
# shaft is pitched 10° then 28°, so the tip finishes ~0.27 in front of the butt
# and the line drops through open air a fifth of the model's width clear of the
# pole. `gap(cord, rod)` is asserted every compile — it is the number that dies
# quietly the moment someone straightens the rod to "tidy the silhouette".
#
# **The line is sized by two failures in opposite directions, and it is a
# narrow window.** Honest monofilament at this scale is sub-pixel: a bobber
# floating unattached in front of a stick, the failure `thornbrush`'s thorns are
# written around, except here the thin element is what the item is named for. So
# it is thickened — but the first pass thickened it to r=0.03, which is *twice*
# the pole's own tip (w=0.032), and the render sheet came back showing two rods:
# a fat pale vertical bar beside a tan curved one, with the bobber hung off the
# bar. r=0.016..0.014 is about a pixel and a half in a 46px slot and reads as
# half the tip's thickness, which is the relationship the eye actually checks.
#
# What buys back the pixels the thinning cost is **colour**. The cord is near
# black (#2b2721) — by a wide margin the darkest thing in the model — so it
# holds against the pale slot background and against the tan pole. The first cut
# had it off-white, which is both invisible on the background and the reason the
# fat version read as a structural member rather than as string.
#
# The taper is 3.3:1 butt to tip and the tip is still 0.032 wide, because a
# genuinely hairline tip drops out of the raster entirely and the rod looks
# snapped off. Same reason `stick` stops at 0.070.

model fishing_rod
  height 0.62
  style chunky

palette
  Grip   #4a3324
  Pole   #c9a05e
  Dark   #8a6635
  Metal  #9aa3ad
  Cord   #2b2721
  Float  #f2ece0
  Red    #cf3b2c

skeleton
  # The butt is off-axis, and only the linter cares. Once the shaft yaws, the
  # rod straddles x=0 lopsidedly — it leaves the butt on one side of the
  # centreline and finishes on the other — and the symmetry lint (rightly) reads
  # a straddling part as one claiming to be symmetric. Sliding the whole model
  # sideways until the *tip* sits on the axis makes the pole honestly one-sided
  # while leaving the line and the float centred, which is the arrangement with
  # no lopsided part in it. Costs nothing downstream: `ItemModels.loadGeometry`
  # recentres X and Z when it loads the glTF, so where this sits in x is not
  # something the game ever sees.
  root butt at (0.105,0,0)
  # The grip is a fifth of the chain and none of the lean: a rod is held
  # straight and bends above the hand. It is also the fraction the game's pose
  # closes the fist at, so it stays a round number.
  bone grip   parent=butt dir=up len=0.22
  # Two shaft bones, not one. The lean has to accumulate — a single 20° bone is
  # a rod leaning like a fence post, where a taper that starts straight and
  # curves off is a rod under tension.
  # The lean carries `yaw` as well as `pitch`, and the yaw is not cosmetic. With
  # a purely forward lean the rod is edge-on from dead ahead and dead behind —
  # two of the four turntable panels showed the bobber sitting *on* the pole
  # with no daylight around it. Throwing the tip sideways as well means no
  # viewing angle collapses the line onto the blank; the worst case is now a
  # diagonal rather than an overlap.
  bone shaft1 parent=grip   dir=up pitch=10 yaw=-8  len=0.42
  bone shaft2 parent=shaft1 dir=up pitch=28 yaw=-26 len=0.42

parts
  # One loft for the whole rod, grip included, banded by material. Authoring the
  # grip as its own part would put a seam where the hand goes and buy nothing:
  # the colour break is the cue, and `material=` on a ring gives a crisp one
  # with split vertices for free.
  loft rod bones=butt..shaft2 material=Pole sides=6
    # `material=` on every grip ring, not just the first. A material named once
    # at ring 0.00 bands only as far as the next ring — the first render sheet
    # came back with a 3%-tall black smudge at the butt and a tan pole above it,
    # i.e. no grip at all.
    ring 0.00 w=0.085 d=0.082 material=Grip
    ring 0.03 w=0.108 d=0.104 material=Grip    # the butt cap flares — a pommel
    ring 0.12 w=0.100 d=0.096 material=Grip
    ring 0.18 w=0.096 d=0.092 material=Grip
    ring 0.21 w=0.078 d=0.075 material=Metal   # the ferrule between grip and blank
    ring 0.24 w=0.075 d=0.072 material=Pole
    ring 0.42 w=0.060 d=0.058
    ring 0.62 w=0.049 d=0.047 material=Dark
    ring 0.84 w=0.040 d=0.038 material=Pole
    ring 1.00 w=0.032 d=0.031
    cap start=flat end=flat

  # The reel. Runs out along +X off the top of the grip, `on=rod` so it is flush
  # against the blank by construction rather than by a radius I would have had
  # to guess. Fat and short: a spool, not a peg.
  # Placed by hand rather than with `on=rod`. `on=` sinks the origin by a third
  # of the base radius, which lands the spool's first ring ~0.02 *past* the
  # centreline, and the lint then (correctly) calls the reel a lopsided central
  # part. The blank is 0.042 across here, so starting at x=0.030 is still inside
  # the tube — flush by arithmetic instead of by construction, and the reel is
  # honestly one-sided.
  loft reel bone=grip at=0.96 offset=(0.030,0,0) dir=side len=0.11 material=Metal sides=6
    ring 0.00 w=0.075 d=0.075
    ring 0.30 w=0.170 d=0.170 material=Dark
    ring 0.72 w=0.170 d=0.170
    ring 1.00 w=0.080 d=0.080 material=Metal
    cap start=flat end=flat

  # The line. Straight down from the tip through the air the forward lean opened
  # up. Four sides, not three: a triangular prism has one facet that goes edge-on
  # and the cord blinks out for part of the drop spin.
  sweep cord bone=shaft2 at=1.00 offset=(0,-0.01,0) dir=down material=Cord sides=4
    seg len=0.17 r=0.016
    seg len=0.16 r=0.015
    seg len=0.14 r=0.014

  # The float. Red below, pale above — the standard two-tone that reads as a
  # bobber and not as a berry. Placed off the tip bone by an explicit drop
  # rather than `on=cord`, which would snap it onto the *side* of the line.
  loft bobber bone=shaft2 at=1.00 offset=(0,-0.455,0) dir=down len=0.19 material=Float sides=6
    ring 0.00 w=0.055 d=0.055
    ring 0.20 w=0.150 d=0.150
    ring 0.42 w=0.180 d=0.180
    ring 0.50 w=0.180 d=0.180 material=Red   # the waterline: pale above, red below
    ring 0.78 w=0.130 d=0.130 material=Red
    ring 1.00 w=0.045 d=0.045 material=Red
    cap start=flat end=flat

checks
  # Stands on its butt.
  assert bottom(rod) < 0.01
  # The line hangs in front of the pole rather than down it. This is the check
  # that keeps the model from being a stick with a bead on it.
  #
  # It cannot be `gap(cord, rod)`: the cord is *tied to* the tip, so its closest
  # approach to the rod is always ~0 by construction and the measurement says
  # nothing about the part that matters. What matters is where the cord's body
  # sits — out in front, past everything the pole occupies at those heights.
  assert zmin(cord) > 0.15
  assert zmin(cord) > zmax(reel)
  assert gap(bobber, rod) > 0.05
  measure clearance zmin(cord) - zmax(reel)
  # The float is well below the tip and well above the butt — dangling, not
  # perched and not dragging on the ground.
  assert top(bobber) < top(rod) * 0.72
  assert bottom(bobber) > top(rod) * 0.30
  # A rod is meant to be narrow across, so `height / width` is not a bound worth
  # having (it reads 6+ and should). The one that matters is depth: the forward
  # lean plus the hanging line have to give the model real front-to-back spread,
  # because that spread *is* the gap the line hangs in. Straighten the rod and
  # this fails before the icon does.
  assert height / depth in 1.9..3.2
  # The reel breaks the outline sideways. Without it the whole model is one
  # stroke and the icon is a stick again.
  assert width(reel) > width(rod) * 0.6
  # The sideways slide the root carries, restated as the two properties it was
  # bought for, so that changing `yaw` without re-deriving the slide fails here
  # rather than in a lint warning three edits later: the pole never crosses the
  # axis, and the line does sit on it.
  assert xmin(rod) > -0.01
  assert abs(xmin(cord) + xmax(cord)) < 0.04
  # The fraction of the model's height the handle occupies — what the game's
  # pose table needs to place `grip`, the point the fist closes at. The handle
  # runs from the butt to the ferrule, so the fist closes at about half of this.
  measure handle len(grip) / height
  assert len(grip) / height in 0.18..0.25
  assert parts == 4
  assert tris < 320
